# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The Modernisation Platform runs a **centralised VPC endpoints hub** in the core network services account. This is a dedicated VPC containing [interface endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints.html) for a wide range of AWS services, shared to consumer accounts rather than duplicated in each VPC.

> The hub currently serves [isolated network accounts](networking-approach.html#isolated-networks) only. If your account uses the standard shared VPC model, see [using endpoints in a standard account](#using-endpoints-in-a-standard-account) below.

## Why we do this

Interface endpoints are charged per endpoint, per availability zone, per hour. Creating an endpoint for every service in every VPC duplicates that cost, and repeats the same DNS and routing configuration in each place.

Isolated network accounts build their own VPC and do not have the shared protected subnets that standard accounts get, so without a central hub each one would need its own full set of endpoints. Running one curated set centrally and reaching it over the [Transit Gateway](networking-approach.html#transit-gateway) means many more services can be offered than would otherwise be practical.

This approach is described in [ADR 0045](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform/blob/main/architecture-decision-record/0045-centralise-vpc-endpoints-with-route53-profiles.md). That ADR is currently marked **Proposed**, and migration of existing endpoint-per-VPC deployments is a separate, phased activity.

## Who it serves

Consumer accounts are listed explicitly in [vpc-endpoints.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform/blob/main/terraform/environments/core-network-services/vpc-endpoints.tf). At the time of writing these are Cloud Platform and Container Platform accounts - the MoJ hosting platforms that run on top of the Modernisation Platform using the isolated network option.

Please check the Terraform for the current list rather than relying on this page.

## How to use it

Access is not automatic. A consumer account needs to:

1. Accept the AWS [Resource Access Manager (RAM)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ram/latest/userguide/what-is.html) share for the `centralised-endpoints` Route53 Profile.
2. Associate that shared [Route53 Profile](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/profiles.html) with the VPCs that need to resolve the endpoint service names.

Once associated, DNS for the supported services resolves to the central endpoints, and traffic routes over the Transit Gateway.

The hub VPC uses the range `10.20.240.0/20`, as recorded in the [CIDR allocation register](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform/blob/main/cidr-allocation.md). Return routes are propagated into the hub's Transit Gateway route table so responses can find their way back.

## Available services

The current list is maintained in [centralised-vpc-endpoints.json](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform/blob/main/terraform/environments/core-network-services/centralised-vpc-endpoints.json). At the time of writing it covers:

`apigateway`, `athena`, `backup`, `cloudtrail`, `config`, `detective`, `dms`, `ec2`, `ec2messages`, `ecr.api`, `ecr.dkr`, `eks`, `eks-auth`, `elasticache`, `email`, `email-smtp`, `events`, `guardduty-data`, `inspector2`, `kinesis-firehose`, `kms`, `lambda`, `logs`, `rds`, `rds-data`, `secretsmanager`, `securityhub`, `sns`, `sqs`, `ssm`, `ssmmessages`, `sts`, `transcribe`, `wafv2`

Again, please check the JSON file for the current list rather than relying on this page.

### Grafana Cloud PDC

In the [core-network-services directory](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform/tree/main/terraform/environments/core-network-services), `vpc-endpoints-grafana-pdc.tf` defines two partner interface endpoints for Grafana Cloud's `prod-gb-south-1` PDC services:

| Endpoint | Service name | Private DNS name | TCP port |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| API | `com.amazonaws.vpce.eu-west-2.vpce-svc-0b12fa07b304f0d79` | `private-datasource-connect-api.eu-west-2.vpce.grafana.net` | 443 |
| SSH gateway | `com.amazonaws.vpce.eu-west-2.vpce-svc-04a3bf37de7c4c8be` | `private-datasource-connect.eu-west-2.vpce.grafana.net` | 22 |

These serve all twelve CP3 consumer VPCs: `cloud-platform-{development,preproduction,nonlive,live}` and `container-platform-{cd,hmpps,laa,octo}-{nonlive,live}`. There is no non-live allow-list. They use the existing hub VPC, Transit Gateway routes and shared Route53 Profile; no endpoints are added to the consumer VPCs.

Each endpoint has a dedicated security group allowing only its own TCP port from those VPCs' primary and secondary pod CIDRs. Keep `grafana_pdc_consumer_cidrs` aligned with [the CP network allocations](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/blob/main/terraform/environments/cloud-platform/network/locals.tf). Terraform rejects a mismatch between its account keys and the existing Profile consumer list.

The hub private-subnet NACL additionally permits these two inbound ports and TCP ephemeral replies to `100.64.0.0/13` and `100.80.0.0/14`: exact aggregates of the twelve pod CIDRs. This supports traffic without node SNAT as well as traffic using the primary VPC CIDRs. Existing AWS endpoint security groups remain unchanged; in particular, they do not gain SSH access.

Supported AZs are discovered for each Grafana service using the hub account's AWS provider. Only matching hub private subnets are selected; no match fails the plan. Do not copy AZ names from another account.

Each private name has a private hosted zone associated with the hub VPC, an apex A alias to its endpoint's regional DNS entry, and an association to the existing shared Profile. Endpoint-managed private DNS is disabled to avoid conflicting with these explicit zones. Public Grafana names are not overridden, and existing AWS endpoint DNS associations are unchanged.

Agents must opt in with `-api-fqdn private-datasource-connect-api.eu-west-2.vpce.grafana.net` and `-gateway-fqdn private-datasource-connect.eu-west-2.vpce.grafana.net`; see [Grafana's PrivateLink guidance](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana-cloud/observe-and-act/connect-externally-hosted/private-data-source-connect/pdc-agent-aws-privatelink/). Provisioning the endpoints does not deploy agents, enable PDC data sources or move OpenSearch into a VPC. OpenSearch Assume Role plus PDC compatibility still needs verification before that rollout, and SigV4 sources need working STS HTTPS access and appropriate IAM permissions.

Before deployment, review the `core-network-services-production` AWS plan for additive Grafana resources only and confirm hub-account service visibility, supported AZs, acceptance requirements and available endpoint, hosted-zone, Profile and NACL quotas. Interface endpoint AZ-hour/data-processing and TGW charges apply; a shared pair avoids a pair per consumer VPC.

After an approved deployment, verify both private names resolve to hub endpoint IPs from live and non-live consumer VPCs, check TCP 443 to the API and TCP 22 to the gateway, and confirm PDC agent connectivity. For un-SNATed pods, verify both secondary CIDR propagation into the hub TGW table and consumer-side routes/NACLs. Check that unsupported ports and non-CP3 sources cannot connect. Local Terraform checks cannot prove these AWS/DNS/network behaviours.

Rollback should remove only the Grafana Profile associations, zones, endpoints and dedicated security resources after agents have been disabled or given an approved alternative path. Do not disassociate the shared Profile or remove the existing AWS endpoints, routes or RAM shares.

## Using endpoints in a standard account

If your account uses the standard shared VPC model, you are **not** onboarded to the hub.

Your VPC already has interface endpoints for EC2, EC2 Messages, SSM and SSM Messages in its [protected subnets](networking-approach.html#protected), plus a gateway endpoint for S3.

If you need an endpoint for another AWS service, we can add it to your VPC using the `additional_endpoints` option in the environments-networks JSON. See [environments-networks json explained](../../user-guide/environments-networks-json-explained.html).

If you think the centralised hub would be a better fit for your use case, please raise it in [#ask-modernisation-platform](https://mojdt.slack.com/archives/C01A7QK5VM1).
